  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}} & =x+3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{5}{2(x+3)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{5}{2(x+3)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne -3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne -3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$",
